        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rasam"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " ",
            "marks": []
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "rasam",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "rasam"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is built on three flavours: the sourness of tamarind, the earthiness of "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "toor dal ",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "toor-dal"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", and the heat of peppercorns. A spoonful of "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "rasam powder",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "rasam-powder"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " brings the elements together. The broth should be very thin and pourable, not thick."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Red lentils work well in place of toor dal. They cook faster and produce a slightly less earthy result, but the rasam is still good."
          }
        ]
      },
